# How much do buyers and sellers gain from a market?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What are consumer and producer surplus? Why is the gain from a market a triangle, not a rectangle?"
next: {"title":"What does a tax destroy?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/17-what-does-a-tax-destroy.cast"}

## Price zero, value huge
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Maps
      - Email
      - Search
    values:
      - 3.6
      - 8.4
      - 17.5
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 25
    icons:
      - map
      - envelope
      - magnifying glass
    y_label: $ thousand to give it up for a year
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 170
      w: 560
      h: 460
sources:
    - id: brynjolfsson2019
      title: Using massive online choice experiments to measure changes in well-being
      authors: E. Brynjolfsson, A. Collis, F. Eggers
      year: 2019
      finding: 'Median US respondent would need $17,530 to give up search engines for a year, $8,414 for email, $3,648 for digital maps (willingness to accept). PNAS 116(15): 7250–7255.'
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.1815663116

    text zero "Price paid: $0" #b5482e cites ["brynjolfsson2019"] x 840 y 600 font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch note work ["Worth − price","= consumer surplus","Price − minimum","= producer surplus"] x 820 y 400 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Price zero, value huge"

How much do buyers and sellers really gain from a market? Start with things you pay nothing for.
    draw zero

B: In a large online experiment, economists asked Americans one question: how much would we have to pay you to give up a service for a whole year?
    draw axes

    pause 0.3

Digital maps: t (@draw bar_1@)he median answer was about three thousand six hundred dollars.

B: Email: about eight thousand four hundred.
    draw bar_2

    ask "Drag the Search bar to your guess, in thousands of dollars a year." on bar_3 store g default "6"
        intro: Now search engines. What would you guess?
        right: Close: you said {g} thousand, and the median was {g.true}.
        wrong: Higher: the median was {g.true} thousand. Most people guess far lower.

The median answer: seventeen and a half thousand dollars a year. For somet (@highlight zero effect underline@)hing whose price is zero.
    highlight bar_3

B: So if we added up what people spend on search, we'd get exactly nothing.
    highlight zero effect circle

Right, and that's the puzzle. The price is what changes hands. What you gain is th (@draw note_box note_line_1@)e gap between what a thing is worth to you and what you pay for it.

B: That gap has a name, I take it.
    highlight note_line_1

Consumer surplus. And selle (@draw note_line_3 note_line_4@)rs have a mirror image: the gap between the price and the least they'd have taken.
    draw note_line_2

B: Then the gain from a whole market is just all those gaps added up?
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

Yes, and to add them up we line the buyers up one by one. Spending tell (@highlight note@)s you what changes hands, not what anyone gains.
    fade bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 axes to 0.3

## Steps into a triangle
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a day (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P* = $5
      q_label: Q* = 100
    regions:
      - consumer_surplus
      - producer_surplus
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 200]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves

    path rect closed #8f887c points [[0,0],[50,0],[50,50],[0,50]] data true style.fill #8f887c style.fill_style wash hidden true
    path tri closed dashed #2f6b8f points [[2,50],[2,92],[50,50]] data true hidden true
    arrow gap1 #b5482e from.data [10,50] to.data [10,84.5] hidden true
    arrow gap2 #b5482e from.data [30,50] to.data [30,66.5] hidden true
    portrait marshall x 500 y 580 width 150 of "Alfred Marshall" cameo true hidden true
    text cs_tag "Consumer surplus" #a87a14 at.data [15,61] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Steps into a triangle"

B: Price isn't value. So let's line up the buyers at Mia's bakery, most eager first. The numbers are illustrative.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D

B: The keenest would pay about nine dollars for a loaf; each one after would pay a little less. That  (@highlight demand_curve@)falling line is the demand curve.
    point at.data [4,90] gesture tap

B: Mia's costs give the supply curve. They cross at five dollars a loaf and a hundred loaves a day.
    draw supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

So the buyers' gain is five dollars times a hundred loaves, that rectangle?
    draw rect

    draw tri

    ask "Which shape is the buyers' gain from this market? Tap it." choose ["rect","tri"] answer tri stage own voice b
        intro: Pick the shape you think is the buyers' gain.
        right: Yes, the triangle: each buyer gains only what the loaf is worth above the price.
        wrong: Not the rectangle: that is the money buyers pay.

B: That rectangle is the money buyers hand to Mia. They lose it, she gets it: a transf (@fade rect to 0.15@)er, not a gain.
    highlight rect

B: Each buyer's gain is how far their value sits above the price. (@draw gap1@) The first gains almost five dollars, the nex (@draw gap2@)t a bit less, and the hundredth gains nothin (@point at.data [50,50] gesture tap@)g: they'd pay exactly five.

Gaps that shrink steadily to zero. Stack them side by side  (@draw cs_region cs_tag@)an (@hide label_CS@)d you get a triangle, not a rectangle.
    erase gap1 gap2 tri rect

B: Alfred Marshall made this idea famous in his 1890 textbook: consumer's surplus. Sellers get th (@erase marshall@)e mirror image: b (@draw ps_region label_PS@)elow the price and above their costs, the producer surplus.
    draw marshall

So the market's gain is both triangles together, and it stops at the last loaf sold. P (@highlight equilibrium_point@)ast that, a loaf costs more to bake than anyone would pay.
    focus cs_region ps_region cs_tag label_PS equilibrium_point

Try it: drag the demand curve to the right, more eager buyers, and watch both triangles grow.
    explore

## How far to trust the triangle
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Marshall would sign that.
    good: You see past the price tag.
    poor: 'Easy to mix up spending and gain: most people do.'
    none: The triangle will wait for you.
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a day (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P* = $5
      q_label: Q* = 100
    regions:
      - consumer_surplus
      - producer_surplus
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 200]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
sources:
    - id: kkt1990
      title: Experimental Tests of the Endowment Effect and the Coase Theorem
      authors: D. Kahneman, J. L. Knetsch, R. H. Thaler
      year: 1990
      finding: 'Students given Cornell coffee mugs asked a median selling price roughly twice the median price others offered to buy them; willingness to accept far exceeds willingness to pay. Journal of Political Economy 98(6): 1325–1348.'
      doi: 10.1086/261737
    - id: kkt1991
      title: 'Anomalies: The Endowment Effect, Loss Aversion, and Status Quo Bias'
      authors: D. Kahneman, J. L. Knetsch, R. H. Thaler
      year: 1991
      finding: 'Summarises the mug markets: median selling prices about twice median buying prices; trading volume less than half of what was expected. Journal of Economic Perspectives 5(1): 193–206.'
    - id: willig1976
      title: Consumer's Surplus Without Apology
      authors: R. D. Willig
      year: 1976
      finding: 'For goods with small income effects, the area under the ordinary demand curve closely approximates the exact welfare measures. American Economic Review 66(4): 589–597.'

    scratch mug cites ["kkt1990","kkt1991"] work ["Mug, Cornell 1990:","sell ≈ 2 × buy"] x 860 y 400 font_size 26 hidden true
    text sure1 "Measure: mostly agreed" #2f6b8f cites ["willig1976"] x 760 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sure2 "Adding people up: contested" #b5482e x 760 y 595 font_size 26 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"P = a - bQ"},{"tex":"CS = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\, Q^* (a - P^*)"},{"tex":"\\text{spending} = P^* Q^*"}] x 820 y 400 font_size 28 hidden true
    scale fish label "Her producer surplus, $" min 0 max 40 value 12 hidden true
    path wta dashed #d0865f points [[2,99.1],[90,20.5]] data true hidden true
    text wta_tag "WTA" #d0865f at.data [93,26] font_size 24 hidden true
    path wta_area closed #d0865f points [[2,92],[2,99.1],[50,56.3],[50,49.1]] data true style.fill #f2c14e style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 hidden true

    card title "How far to trust the triangle"

So the gain from a market is the triangle between the curves, not the money that changes hands.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar cs_region label_CS ps_region label_PS parallel true

B: But a triangle is only as good as the curve on top of it. And that search-engine number answered an odd question: what would you accept to give it up?
    highlight demand_curve

    pause 0.3

Fair. People often ask more to give a thing up than they'd pay to get it.
    draw mug_box mug_line_1

B: In a classic 1990 experiment, students handed a coffee mug asked roug (@draw mug_line_2@)hly twice as much to sell it as others offered to buy it.

Ask that question (@draw wta wta_tag@) about bread, and the curve sits higher and the (@draw wta_area@) triangle grows. So which triangle is the real gain?

    erase mug

B: And there's a quieter assumption: a dollar counts the same whoever holds it. A rich buyer's eagerness weighs as much as a poor buyer's need.
    point at.data [6,92] gesture circle

How sure are we? For everyday goods that take a small slice of income, the triangle is a good  (@draw sure1@)measure: mostly agreed.
    erase wta wta_tag wta_area

B: Adding those dollars up across rich and poor as if they were the sa (@draw sure2@)me is another matter: that part is contested.

    draw mathq

    ask "Want the algebra behind the triangle?" on mathq stage own

@math

    hide mathq

    erase sure1 sure2

Write demand as a straight line: from a, the price buyers will pay falls by b for every extra loaf.
    draw math_box math_line_1

B: The surplus is a triangle: half the base, Q star, times the height, a minus P star.
    draw math_line_2

Spending is the whole rectangle, P star times Q star, and it isn't surplus at all: it just moves from buyer to seller.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@test

    hide mathq

    hide sure1 sure2

    erase axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar cs_region label_CS ps_region label_PS

    draw fish

    ask "A fisher would sell a crate of cod for at least $20, and gets $32. What is her producer surplus?" on fish store f
        right: Yes: {f.true}, the price minus the least she'd have taken.
        wrong: It's {f.true}: thirty-two minus the twenty she'd have taken anyway.

    erase fish

    quiz "Wikipedia is free to read. What does its zero price tell us about what readers gain from it?" voice b
        right: Right: a zero price only says nothing changes hands; the gain is all surplus.
        wrong: Think of the search engines: the price is what changes hands, not what you gain.
        + Nothing: the gain is its worth above a zero price
        * Readers gain nothing, since they pay nothing
        * The gain equals the donations it receives

    quiz "A concert sells out at $60 a ticket. Which buyer gains least?"
        right: Yes: the fan at $60 is the last buyer, with a gap of zero.
        wrong: Surplus is the gap between what it's worth to you and the price.
        * The fan who would have paid $200
        + The fan who would have paid just $60
        * Every buyer gains the same

    quiz "The harbour council asks what a new ferry is worth to Harbourtown. Which number comes closest?" voice b
        right: Yes: both surpluses. The fares just move money from riders to the operator.
        wrong: Revenue is what changes hands. What is left over on each side?
        * The ticket revenue
        + Riders' value above the fare, plus the operator's margin over cost
        * The cost of building it

So do markets work? Part of the answer is this triangle: every trade where something is worth more to the buyer than it costs to make, and a gain that never shows up in the price.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar cs_region label_CS ps_region label_PS parallel true

